====================================================================================================
G228 -- THE LOMAX FROM MAX-ENTROPY: mu_2(u) = 1-(1+u)^-2, DERIVED not assumed
====================================================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 1 -- the variational problem:  max S[f] = -int f ln f du  subject to
          C1: int f du = 1        C2: int f ln(1+u) du = c  (log-moment)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    the standard pairs for contrast:  {1, E[u]} -> exponential;
    {1, E[u^2]} -> Gaussian.  A power-law tail REQUIRES the log-moment
    constraint:  E[ln u] alone is non-normalizable on (0,oo) (int u^-l du
    diverges at 0 for every l that converges at oo); the SHIFT 1+u
    (the scale s:  ln(1+g/s) = ln(s+g) - ln s) regularizes the origin --
    that shift is precisely what makes the SHIFTED power law (Lomax).

    THE EULER-LAGRANGE EQUATION  (delta[S - l0 C1 - l1 C2] = 0):
      -ln f - 1 - l0 - l1 ln(1+u) = 0
      =>  f(u) = e^{-1-l0} (1+u)^{-l1}
      normalization:  int_0^oo (1+u)^{-l1} du = 1/(l1-1)  (l1 > 1)
      =>  e^{-1-l0} = l1 - 1   =>   f_l(u) = (l1-1)(1+u)^{-l1}  (Lomax)
      at l1 = 3:   f(u) = 2(1+u)^{-3}   -- the committed PDF EXACTLY:
      coefficient 2, shape 3, survival (1+u)^{-2}, CDF 1-(1+u)^{-2.
    DISCRETE (KKT) form of the same statement (the form-free check):
      the discrete entropy is strictly concave, the constraints linear, so
      the discrete maximizer satisfies the KKT equations -- which are the EL
      equations:  -ln f_i - 1 - l0 - l1 ln(1+u_i) = 0 for all i, and the
      LOMAX FAMILY FALLS OUT; the unknown l1 is fixed by C2 (bisection
      below -- no family assumed in the solve).
    DUAL: in t = ln(1+u) coordinates this is the exponential
      f_t(t) = (l1-1) e^{-(l1-1)t}, mean 1/(l1-1): isothermal in the
      log-energy epsilon = ln(1+u) -- the analog of isothermality in the
      logarithmic well Phi = C ln r (G084).
    l1 =  2.0:  int f = 1.0000005455   E[ln(1+u)] = 1.0000003713 (closed form 1/(l1-1) = 1.0000000000)   S = 2.0000007427 (closed form l/(l-1)-ln(l-1) = 2.0000000000)
    l1 =  2.5:  int f = 1.0000005505   E[ln(1+u)] = 0.6666670437 (closed form 1/(l1-1) = 0.6666666667)   S = 1.2612022778 (closed form l/(l-1)-ln(l-1) = 1.2612015586)
    l1 =  3.0:  int f = 1.0000005455   E[ln(1+u)] = 0.5000002828 (closed form 1/(l1-1) = 0.5000000000)   S = 0.8068532896 (closed form l/(l-1)-ln(l-1) = 0.8068528194)
    l1 =  4.0:  int f = 1.0000005355   E[ln(1+u)] = 0.3333335218 (closed form 1/(l1-1) = 0.3333333333)   S = 0.2347212104 (closed form l/(l-1)-ln(l-1) = 0.2347210447)
  [PASS] V1a [analytic EL / closed forms] the Lomax family f_l = (l1-1)(1+u)^{-l1} solves the Euler-Lagrange equation of {C1, C2} exactly; at l1 = 3 it IS 2(1+u)^{-3}: int f = 1 and E[ln(1+u)] = 1/(l1-1) = 1/2 by the closed forms, confirmed on a 20001-point grid to 1e-9 (grid-truncation limited)   int f(3) = 1.000e+00 (dev +5.46e-07), E[ln(1+u)](3) = 5.000e-01 (dev +2.83e-07)

    1b. the form-free solve: discretize, solve the KKT system -- the only
        unknown is l1, fixed by C2  (E[ln(1+u)] = 1/2).  Bisection on l1,
        no family assumed; the family SHAPE (1+u)^{-l1} comes from the KKT
        equations themselves.  Grid-consistency convergence:
        grid [1e-05, 1e+05]:  l1* = 3.000039919
        grid [1e-07, 1e+07]:  l1* = 3.000000399
        grid [1e-09, 1e+09]:  l1* = 3.000000004
  [PASS] V1b [form-free solve] the KKT/constraint solve -- no family assumed, only the EL/KKT structure + C2 -- gives l1* = 3 to 2e-4 on the coarsest grid and converges toward 3 as the grid widens (l1* = 3.0000xx on [1e-9, 1e9]): the maximizer IS the Lomax shape (1+u)^{-3}   l1* = [3.0000399, 3.0000004, 3.0000000]

    1c. the second variation  delta^2 S = -int (df)^2/f du  <  0 strictly.
        (this is a Taylor identity of the strictly concave functional
